<section xml:id="sec-course-track-and-drift" component="core" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Course, Track and Drift</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.0 Measurements and Geometry
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        2.7 Navigation
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Determine the course, track and drift of a body moving in a flowing fluid
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        12
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        2 lessons of 40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- Planning notes (from the Combined Mathematics tab of the Grade 12 Book Structure Mapping sheet)
         Content summary: Course, track and drift of a body moving in a flowing fluid (e.g. a boat in a current, aircraft in wind).
         Suggested activity: Learners float a paper boat across a stream or water trough and observe how the current carries it off course, then draw the velocity triangle.
         Interactive (JSXGraph): Boat velocity and current velocity as draggable vectors; the resultant (track) and drift angle update live. -->

    <!-- Section notes (2026-11)
         SLOs: Core (f); also (g) apply and (h) role (safety at sea and in the air). Core only (section root). Lessons 5.1 and 5.2 as subsections.
         Activity: as planning notes (paper boats across a water trough; the velocity triangle)
         JSXGraph: fig-velocity-triangle (assets/jsxgraph/velocity-triangle.js), as planning notes: draggable course and current tips, live track and drift; a "hold track" checkbox for lesson 5.2
         STACK: assets/stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/05-course-track-and-drift/ -->

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 5.1 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-course-track-and-drift">
        <title>Course, Track and Drift</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> course, track, drift, current and wind; the velocity triangle; the difference between a current flowing <em>towards</em> a direction and a wind blowing <em>from</em> one.</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find the ground speed, drift angle and track when the current is at right angles to the course, then at any angle (cosine and sine rules).</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> boats crossing rivers, dhows in a current, light aircraft in a wind.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners watch a floating body carried off course by moving water, then draw the velocity triangle to explain what they saw.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Prepare a long water trough (or a gutter, or a shallow stream) with a gentle flow from a jug or hosepipe; paper boats, a stopwatch and a metre rule per group. Remind learners about safety near water: no learner wades into a stream. Do not use the words <q>course</q> or <q>track</q> until the Structured Instruction.</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-paper-boats-across-a-trough">
            <title>Paper Boats Across a Trough</title>
            <statement>
                <p>Each group makes a paper boat and pushes it straight across a trough of water.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>Push the boat straight across still water and time it. Then do the same while the water is flowing along the trough. What happens?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>It takes about the same time to cross, but it lands downstream of the point straight opposite: the water carries it along while it crosses.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>In one trough the boat moves across the water at <m>0.5\,\text{m/s}</m> and the water flows along the trough at <m>0.4\,\text{m/s}</m>. Draw the boat's velocity and the water's velocity as arrows to scale (<m>1\,\text{cm}</m> for <m>0.1\,\text{m/s}</m>), the second starting at the tip of the first. Join the start to the end. How long is this arrow, and what speed does it show?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Arrows of <m>5\,\text{cm}</m> across and <m>4\,\text{cm}</m> along; the joining arrow is about <m>6.4\,\text{cm}</m>, a speed of about <m>0.64\,\text{m/s}</m> (<m>\sqrt{0.5^2 + 0.4^2} = 0.640</m>).</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Measure the angle between the direction the boat points and the direction it actually goes.</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>About <m>39^\circ</m> (<m>\tan^{-1} 0.8 = 38.7^\circ</m>).</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>The trough is <m>1.5\,\text{m}</m> wide. How long does the boat take to cross, and how far downstream does it land?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p><m>1.5 \div 0.5 = 3\,\text{s}</m>; in <m>3\,\text{s}</m> the water carries it <m>3 \times 0.4 = 1.2\,\text{m}</m> downstream.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To name the arrows of the learners' triangle and to state how to find the ground speed, drift and track.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> the boat's arrow is the course, the water's arrow is the current, and the joining arrow is the track. The angle in part (c) is the drift.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Course velocity <m>+</m> current velocity <m>=</m> track velocity. These are vectors: the speeds do not simply add.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Read every direction carefully: currents are given by where they flow <em>towards</em>, winds by where they blow <em>from</em>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-course-track-and-drift">
            <title>Course, Track and Drift</title>
            <p>Directions are three-figure bearings, measured clockwise from north, from <m>000^\circ</m> up to (but not including) <m>360^\circ</m>.</p>
            <p>
                <ul>
                    <li><p>The <term>course</term> is the direction in which a boat or aircraft is steered (pointed). Its speed through the water or air (the <em>water speed</em> or <em>airspeed</em>) goes with it.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A <term>current</term> is moving water, and a <term>wind</term> moving air. A current flowing towards <m>120^\circ</m> carries a boat towards <m>120^\circ</m>. A wind blowing from <m>300^\circ</m> pushes an aircraft towards <m>120^\circ</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The <term>track</term> is the direction in which the boat or aircraft actually moves over the ground or the sea bed. Its speed along the track is the <term>ground speed</term>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>The <term>drift</term> (or drift angle) is the angle between the course and the track.</p></li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>The course is where the boat points; the track is where it goes. In the <term>velocity triangle</term> the current arrow starts at the tip of the course arrow, and the track arrow (drawn with a double head) joins the start to the end:
                <md>\text{course velocity} + \text{current velocity} = \text{track velocity}</md>.
                Solve the triangle with Pythagoras and <m>\tan</m> when the current is at right angles to the course, and with the cosine and sine rules otherwise.</p>
            <p>Sailors also use <q>set and drift</q> to mean the direction and speed of a current. In this book <q>drift</q> always means the drift angle.</p>
        </insight>

        <figure xml:id="fig-velocity-triangle-labels">
            <caption>A velocity triangle for a dhow steered on a course of <m>040^\circ</m> at <m>8\,\text{knots}</m> in a current flowing towards <m>160^\circ</m> at <m>3\,\text{knots}</m>.</caption>
            <image width="55%">
                <shortdescription>A velocity triangle. From the start O, a single-headed arrow labelled course, 8 knots, points on a bearing of 040 degrees. From its tip, an arrow labelled current, 3 knots, points towards 160 degrees. A double-headed arrow labelled track joins O to the end. The drift angle d is marked at O between the course and the track. A north line is drawn at O.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.55, >=stealth]
% SET: O start; B tip of course (bearing 040, 8 units); C tip of current (towards 160, 3 units)
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (5.142,6.128);
\coordinate (C) at (6.168,3.309);
% DRAW: north line
\draw[thin] (O) -- (0,7.5) node[above] {N};
% DRAW: course (single head), current (single head), track (double head)
\draw[->, thick] (O) -- (B) node[midway, sloped, above] {course, 8 knots};
\draw[->, thick, dashed] (B) -- (C) node[midway, right] {current, 3 knots};
\draw[->>, very thick] (O) -- (C) node[midway, sloped, below] {track};
% MARK: drift angle at O between course (040) and track (061.8)
\draw (0,0) ++(50:1.6) arc (50:28.2:1.6);
\node at (40:2.2) {$d$};
% MARK: bearing of the course from north
\draw (0,0) ++(90:1.0) arc (90:50:1.0);
\node at (72:1.5) {\small $40^\circ$};
% LABEL
\fill (O) circle (2pt) node[below left] {O};
\end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <figure xml:id="fig-velocity-triangle">
            <caption>The velocity triangle. Drag the tip of the course arrow and the tip of the current arrow; the double arrow is the track. Tick <q>hold track</q> to find the course to steer for a chosen track.</caption>
            <interactive xml:id="interactive-velocity-triangle" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/velocity-triangle.js">
                <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-velocity-triangle" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
                <instructions>
                    <p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><p>Drag the point <alert>B</alert> (the tip of the course arrow from O) and the point <alert>C</alert> (the tip of the current arrow from B). The readout gives the course, the current, the track, the ground speed and the drift angle. One grid square is <m>0.1</m> units of speed.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Check the activity: put B at <m>0.5</m> north of O (course <m>000^\circ</m>) and C at <m>0.4</m> east of B. The readout gives a ground speed of <m>0.6</m> (more exactly <m>0.64</m>) and a drift of <m>38.7^\circ</m>.</p></li>
                            <li><p>Point the course where you want to go, then tick <alert>hold track</alert>. That direction becomes the required track (dashed line); the current and the boat's speed are fixed, and B now moves on a circle. Turn B until C lies on the dashed line (the readout says <q>on track</q>), and read how far the course is from the track: that is the angle you must steer into the current.</p></li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </instructions>
            </interactive>
        </figure>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-crossing-the-tana-river">
            <title>Crossing the Tana River</title>
            <statement>
                <p>At Garsen a boat is steered straight across the Tana River at <m>2.4\,\text{m/s}</m> through the water. The river flows at <m>0.7\,\text{m/s}</m> and is <m>120\,\text{m}</m> wide. Find the ground speed, the drift angle, the time to cross and how far downstream the boat lands.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The current is at right angles to the course, so the velocity triangle is right-angled with the ground speed as its hypotenuse.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\text{ground speed} \amp = \sqrt{2.4^2 + 0.7^2} = \sqrt{6.25} = 2.5\,\text{m/s}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\tan d \amp = \frac{0.7}{2.4}, \quad d = 16.3^\circ</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The speed across the river is still <m>2.4\,\text{m/s}</m>, so the crossing takes <m>120 \div 2.4 = 50\,\text{s}</m>. In that time the current carries the boat <m>50 \times 0.7 = 35\,\text{m}</m> downstream.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-wind-on-a-flight-from-wilson">
            <title>Wind on a Flight from Wilson Airport</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A light aircraft leaves Wilson Airport, Nairobi, on a course of <m>090^\circ</m> with an airspeed of <m>160\,\text{knots}</m>. A wind of <m>28\,\text{knots}</m> blows from <m>000^\circ</m>. Find the ground speed, the drift angle and the track.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>A wind from <m>000^\circ</m> (from the north) blows towards <m>180^\circ</m>, at right angles to the course.
                    <md>
                        <mrow>\text{ground speed} \amp = \sqrt{160^2 + 28^2} = 162.4\,\text{knots}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\tan d \amp = \frac{28}{160}, \quad d = 9.9^\circ</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The wind pushes the aircraft south of east, so the track is <m>090^\circ + 9.9^\circ = 099.9^\circ</m>.</p>
                <p>Check with column vectors (east, north): <m>\begin{pmatrix} 160 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -28 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 160 \\ -28 \end{pmatrix}</m>, which has length <m>162.4</m> and points <m>9.9^\circ</m> south of east.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-a-dhow-off-lamu">
            <title>A Dhow off Lamu</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A dhow off Lamu is steered on a course of <m>040^\circ</m> at <m>8\,\text{knots}</m>. A current flows towards <m>160^\circ</m> at <m>3\,\text{knots}</m>. Find the ground speed, the drift angle and the track (see <xref ref="fig-velocity-triangle-labels"/>).</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The directions <m>040^\circ</m> and <m>160^\circ</m> differ by <m>120^\circ</m>, so inside the triangle the angle between the course arrow and the current arrow is <m>180^\circ - 120^\circ = 60^\circ</m>.</p>
                <p><alert>Step 1: cosine rule.</alert> <md>\text{ground speed}^2 = 8^2 + 3^2 - 2 \times 8 \times 3 \cos 60^\circ = 64 + 9 - 24 = 49</md> so the ground speed is <m>7\,\text{knots}</m>.</p>
                <p><alert>Step 2: sine rule.</alert> <md>\sin d = \frac{3 \sin 60^\circ}{7}, \quad d = 21.8^\circ</md></p>
                <p><alert>Step 3: track.</alert> The current pushes the dhow clockwise from its course, so the track is <m>040^\circ + 21.8^\circ = 061.8^\circ</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>36.8</c>. Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign. Choose N, S, E or W from each list.</p>

