<exercise xml:id="ex-true-or-false-course-track-and-drift">
    <!--<title></title>-->
    <stack label="stk-true-or-false-course-track-and-drift" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/05-course-track-and-drift/True-or-false-course-track-and-drift.xml"/>
</exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-course-track-and-drift">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-boat-across-at-right-angles">
                <statement><p>A boat is steered straight across a river at <m>3.5\,\text{m/s}</m> through the water. The current is <m>1.2\,\text{m/s}</m>. Find the ground speed and the drift angle.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>3.7\,\text{m/s}</m>; <m>18.9^\circ</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>\sqrt{3.5^2 + 1.2^2} = \sqrt{13.69} = 3.7\,\text{m/s}</m>; <m>\tan d = 1.2 \div 3.5</m>, <m>d = 18.9^\circ</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-aircraft-course-180">
                <statement><p>An aircraft flies on a course of <m>180^\circ</m> with an airspeed of <m>210\,\text{knots}</m>. A wind of <m>36\,\text{knots}</m> blows from <m>090^\circ</m>. Find the ground speed, the drift angle and the track.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>213.1\,\text{knots}</m>; <m>9.7^\circ</m>; <m>189.7^\circ</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The wind blows towards <m>270^\circ</m>, at right angles to the course. <m>\sqrt{210^2 + 36^2} = 213.1\,\text{knots}</m>; <m>\tan d = 36 \div 210</m>, <m>d = 9.7^\circ</m>. Pushed west of south, so the track is <m>180^\circ + 9.7^\circ = 189.7^\circ</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-ship-course-300">
                <statement><p>A ship is steered on a course of <m>300^\circ</m> at <m>14\,\text{knots}</m>. A current flows towards <m>030^\circ</m> at <m>5\,\text{knots}</m>. Find the ground speed, the drift angle and the track.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>14.9\,\text{knots}</m>; <m>19.7^\circ</m>; <m>319.7^\circ</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>030^\circ</m> is <m>90^\circ</m> clockwise from <m>300^\circ</m>. <m>\sqrt{14^2 + 5^2} = 14.9\,\text{knots}</m>; <m>\tan d = 5 \div 14</m>, <m>d = 19.7^\circ</m>; track <m>300^\circ + 19.7^\circ = 319.7^\circ</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-ship-current-at-60-degrees">
                <statement><p>A ship is steered on a course of <m>120^\circ</m> at <m>9\,\text{knots}</m>. A current flows towards <m>060^\circ</m> at <m>4\,\text{knots}</m>. Find the ground speed, the drift angle and the track.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>11.5\,\text{knots}</m>; <m>17.5^\circ</m>; <m>102.5^\circ</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The directions differ by <m>60^\circ</m>, so the angle inside the triangle is <m>120^\circ</m>. <m>\text{ground speed}^2 = 9^2 + 4^2 - 2 \times 9 \times 4 \cos 120^\circ = 133</m>, ground speed <m>11.5\,\text{knots}</m>. <m>\sin d = 4 \sin 120^\circ \div 11.53</m>, <m>d = 17.5^\circ</m>. The current pushes anticlockwise, so the track is <m>120^\circ - 17.5^\circ = 102.5^\circ</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-crossing-time-and-distance">
                <title>Challenge: Time to Cross</title>
                <statement><p>A river is <m>80\,\text{m}</m> wide. A boat is steered straight across at <m>1.6\,\text{m/s}</m> through the water, and the current is <m>0.9\,\text{m/s}</m>. How long does the crossing take, and how far downstream does the boat land?</p></statement>
                <hint><p>The current does not change the time to cross.</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>50\,\text{s}</m>; <m>45\,\text{m}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>Across the river the boat moves at <m>1.6\,\text{m/s}</m>: <m>80 \div 1.6 = 50\,\text{s}</m>. Downstream it moves at <m>0.9\,\text{m/s}</m> for <m>50\,\text{s}</m>: <m>45\,\text{m}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-course-track-and-drift">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>The activity and <xref ref="example-crossing-the-tana-river"/>. Give a card: <q>course + current = track</q>, with a right-angled triangle labelled. Assign <xref ref="ex-boat-across-at-right-angles"/> and <xref ref="ex-aircraft-course-180"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-boat-across-at-right-angles"/> to <xref ref="ex-ship-current-at-60-degrees"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-crossing-time-and-distance"/>. Extension: show that when the current is at right angles to the course, <m>\tan d</m> = current speed <m>\div</m> boat speed. (Answer: in the right-angled triangle the current is opposite <m>d</m> and the course is adjacent to it.)</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Closing question for the class: why must a pilot flying from Wilson Airport know the wind before take-off?</p>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ============================================================ Lesson 5.2 -->
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-steering-a-course">
        <title>Steering a Course</title>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Upon completion of this lesson, learners will be able to:
            <ol>
                <li><p><alert>Know:</alert> to make good a required track, a boat must be steered partly into the current (an aircraft partly into the wind).</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Do:</alert> find the course to steer and the ground speed, first when the current is at right angles to the track, then at any angle (sine rule).</p></li>
                <li><p><alert>Apply:</alert> ferries, cargo ships and flights; safety on the water and in the air.</p></li>
            </ol>
        </p>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
            <dl>
                <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>14 minutes</p></li>
                <li><title>Objective</title><p>Learners find, by experiment with the interactive, where to point a boat so that it lands straight opposite its starting point.</p></li>
                <li><title>Teacher's Role</title><p>Use <xref ref="fig-velocity-triangle"/> with <q>hold track</q> ticked. After the activity, discuss why ferry pilots, fishermen and aircraft pilots check the currents and winds before they set out (safety and security).</p></li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

        <activity xml:id="act-reaching-the-jetty">
            <title>Reaching the Jetty</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A ferry crosses a channel to a jetty straight opposite. It moves at <m>2.8\,\text{m/s}</m> through the water, and the current flows at <m>0.7\,\text{m/s}</m>.</p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>In <xref ref="fig-velocity-triangle"/>, put B at <m>2.8</m> north of O (straight across the channel) and C at <m>0.7</m> east of B (the current). Tick <alert>hold track</alert>, then turn B until C lies on the dashed line. At what angle upstream must the ferry be pointed?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>About <m>14.5^\circ</m> upstream (<m>\sin d = 0.7 \div 2.8 = 0.25</m>).</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>Why is the ground speed now less than <m>2.8\,\text{m/s}</m>?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>Part of the ferry's speed through the water is used to cancel the current, so less is left for crossing: <m>\sqrt{2.8^2 - 0.7^2} = 2.7\,\text{m/s}</m>.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>What happens if the current is faster than the ferry?</p>
                            <solution component="lesson-plan"><p>It cannot go straight across: however it is pointed, the current carries it downstream.</p></solution>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </activity>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Structured Instruction</title>
            <p>
                <dl>
                    <li><title>Suggested Time</title><p>8 minutes</p></li>
                    <li><title>Objective</title><p>To set out how to find the course to steer for a required track.</p></li>
                </dl>
            </p>
            <paragraphs>
                <title>Key Takeaways &amp; Teacher Connection</title>
                <ol>
                    <li><p><alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> in the activity the track was fixed and the course was unknown: the opposite of lesson 5.1.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Draw the track line first. Draw the current arrow from the start, then the course arrow (its length is the boat's speed) from the tip of the current arrow to meet the track line.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Use the sine rule for the angle between the course and the track; steer that angle on the upstream (upwind) side of the track.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </paragraphs>
        </axiom>

        <insight xml:id="insight-steering-a-course">
            <title>Steering a Course</title>
            <p>When the track is known and the course is not, draw the current arrow from the start, then the course arrow from its tip so that its length is the boat's speed and its end lies on the track line. If <m>\alpha</m> is the angle between the track and the direction of the current, the sine rule gives the drift angle <m>d</m>:
                <md>\sin d = \frac{\text{current speed} \times \sin \alpha}{\text{boat speed}}</md>.
                Steer <m>d</m> degrees on the upstream (upwind) side of the track. The ground speed is then
                <md>\text{ground speed} = \text{boat speed} \times \cos d + \text{current speed} \times \cos \alpha</md>
                (the parts of the two velocities along the track). When the current is at right angles to the track, <m>\sin d = \text{current speed} \div \text{boat speed}</m> and the ground speed is <m>\sqrt{\text{boat speed}^2 - \text{current speed}^2}</m>.</p>
        </insight>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

        <example xml:id="example-straight-across-likoni">
            <title>Straight Across the Likoni Channel</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A ferry crosses the Likoni channel at Mombasa at <m>2.8\,\text{m/s}</m> through the water. The current flows at <m>0.7\,\text{m/s}</m> along the channel. At what angle upstream must the ferry be steered to go straight across, and what is its ground speed?</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The current is at right angles to the required track. <md>\sin d = \frac{0.7}{2.8} = 0.25, \quad d = 14.5^\circ</md> upstream. The ground speed is <m>\sqrt{2.8^2 - 0.7^2} = \sqrt{7.35} = 2.7\,\text{m/s}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-flying-due-north">
            <title>Flying Due North</title>
            <statement>
                <p>A pilot must fly on a track of <m>000^\circ</m> (due north). The airspeed is <m>150\,\text{knots}</m> and a wind of <m>25\,\text{knots}</m> blows from <m>270^\circ</m>. Find the course to steer and the ground speed.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>A wind from <m>270^\circ</m> (from the west) blows towards <m>090^\circ</m>, at right angles to the track. <md>\sin d = \frac{25}{150}, \quad d = 9.6^\circ</md> The wind pushes east, so steer west of north: course <m>360^\circ - 9.6^\circ = 350.4^\circ</m>. Ground speed <m>\sqrt{150^2 - 25^2} = 147.9\,\text{knots}</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="example-making-good-a-track-of-060">
            <title>Making Good a Track of <m>060^\circ</m></title>
            <statement>
                <p>A cargo ship must make good a track of <m>060^\circ</m>. Its speed through the water is <m>12\,\text{knots}</m>, and a current flows towards <m>180^\circ</m> at <m>3.5\,\text{knots}</m>. Find the course to steer and the ground speed.</p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>The angle between the track <m>060^\circ</m> and the current <m>180^\circ</m> is <m>\alpha = 120^\circ</m>.</p>
                <p><alert>Step 1: sine rule.</alert> <md>\sin d = \frac{3.5 \sin 120^\circ}{12}, \quad d = 14.6^\circ</md></p>
                <p><alert>Step 2: course.</alert> The current pushes the ship clockwise from the track, so steer anticlockwise: course <m>060^\circ - 14.6^\circ = 045.4^\circ</m>.</p>
                <p><alert>Step 3: ground speed.</alert> <md>12 \cos 14.6^\circ + 3.5 \cos 120^\circ = 11.61 - 1.75 = 9.9\,\text{knots}</md></p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <note xml:id="note-safety-on-the-water">
            <title>Safety on the Water</title>
            <p>Currents are strongest at river mouths, in narrow channels such as the Likoni channel and when the tide turns. Anyone crossing water in a small boat should wear a life jacket and check the current first: a boat that cannot beat the current will be carried away however it is steered. Pilots work out a wind-corrected course before every flight so that they reach their destination with enough fuel.</p>
        </note>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" />

        <p>Type decimals with a point, for example <c>36.8</c>. Type angles in degrees as numbers, without the degree sign. Choose N, S, E or W from each list.</p>

<exercise xml:id="ex-course-to-steer-for-a-required-track">
    <!--<title></title>-->
    <stack label="stk-course-to-steer-for-a-required-track" source="stack/top/2-measurement-and-geometry/13-navigation/05-course-track-and-drift/Course-to-steer-for-a-required-track.xml"/>
</exercise>

        <exercises xml:id="exercises-steering-a-course">
            <exercise xml:id="ex-straight-across-1-6">
                <statement><p>A boat moves at <m>3.4\,\text{m/s}</m> in still water. The current is <m>1.6\,\text{m/s}</m>. At what angle upstream must the boat be steered to go straight across, and what is its ground speed?</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>28.1^\circ</m>; <m>3.0\,\text{m/s}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p><m>\sin d = 1.6 \div 3.4</m>, <m>d = 28.1^\circ</m>; <m>\sqrt{3.4^2 - 1.6^2} = \sqrt{9} = 3.0\,\text{m/s}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-track-090-wind-from-north">
                <statement><p>A pilot must fly on a track of <m>090^\circ</m> with an airspeed of <m>240\,\text{knots}</m>. A wind of <m>48\,\text{knots}</m> blows from <m>000^\circ</m>. Find the course to steer and the ground speed.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>078.5^\circ</m>; <m>235.2\,\text{knots}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The wind blows towards <m>180^\circ</m>, at right angles to the track. <m>\sin d = 48 \div 240</m>, <m>d = 11.5^\circ</m>; steer north of east: <m>090^\circ - 11.5^\circ = 078.5^\circ</m>. Ground speed <m>\sqrt{240^2 - 48^2} = 235.2\,\text{knots}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-track-135-current-045">
                <statement><p>A ship must make good a track of <m>135^\circ</m> at <m>15\,\text{knots}</m> through the water. A current flows towards <m>045^\circ</m> at <m>3\,\text{knots}</m>. Find the course to steer and the ground speed.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>146.5^\circ</m>; <m>14.7\,\text{knots}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The current is at right angles to the track and pushes anticlockwise. <m>\sin d = 3 \div 15</m>, <m>d = 11.5^\circ</m>; steer clockwise: <m>135^\circ + 11.5^\circ = 146.5^\circ</m>. Ground speed <m>\sqrt{15^2 - 3^2} = 14.7\,\text{knots}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-track-200-current-300">
                <statement><p>A ship must make good a track of <m>200^\circ</m> at <m>11\,\text{knots}</m> through the water. A current flows towards <m>300^\circ</m> at <m>2.5\,\text{knots}</m>. Find the course to steer and the ground speed.</p></statement>
                <answer><p><m>187.1^\circ</m>; <m>10.3\,\text{knots}</m></p></answer>
                <solution><p>The angle between the track and the current is <m>100^\circ</m>. <m>\sin d = 2.5 \sin 100^\circ \div 11</m>, <m>d = 12.9^\circ</m>. The current pushes clockwise, so steer anticlockwise: <m>200^\circ - 12.9^\circ = 187.1^\circ</m>. Ground speed <m>11 \cos 12.9^\circ + 2.5 \cos 100^\circ = 10.72 - 0.43 = 10.3\,\text{knots}</m>.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
            <exercise xml:id="ex-current-too-strong">
                <title>Challenge: A Current Too Strong</title>
                <statement><p>A fisherman's dugout moves at <m>1.1\,\text{m/s}</m> in still water. After heavy rain the river flows at <m>1.3\,\text{m/s}</m>. Explain why the dugout cannot reach the point straight opposite.</p></statement>
                <hint><p>Work out <m>\sin d</m>.</p></hint>
                <answer><p><m>\sin d = 1.3 \div 1.1 = 1.18</m>, which is impossible.</p></answer>
                <solution><p>To go straight across, <m>\sin d = 1.3 \div 1.1 = 1.18</m> (to 2 d.p.). The sine of an angle is never more than <m>1</m>, so no course works: the current is faster than the dugout, which is always carried downstream.</p></solution>
            </exercise>
        </exercises>

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Differentiation</title>
            <table xml:id="table-differentiation-steering-a-course">
                <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>The activity and <xref ref="example-straight-across-likoni"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-straight-across-1-6"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-straight-across-1-6"/> to <xref ref="ex-track-200-current-300"/>.</cell></row>
                    <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-current-too-strong"/>. Extension, for discussion only: if the current is too strong to go straight across, how should the dugout be pointed to be carried as little as possible downstream? (Answer in words: point it partly upstream, so that the course is at right angles to the resulting track.)</cell></row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
        </axiom>

        <p component="lesson-plan">Closing question for the class: what should a fisherman on Lake Victoria check before setting out in a small boat, and why?</p>

        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-methods.ptx" />
        <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/reflection.ptx"/>
    </subsection>
</section>
